| 3 | 3 | true | true |
| 4 | 4 | true | true |
| 5 | 5 | true | true |
| 6 | 6 | true | true |
| 7 | 7 | true | true |

Minimum distance 3; single-error cases 112; recovered 112; codeword counts by weight0..7: 1 0 0 7 7 0 0 1.
\image(figures/hamming_single.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Seven original single-flip cases give the identity line. The exhaustive audit verifies the complete 16-message code, rather than generalizing from one nibble.)
\alttext(Each of seven single-flip positions maps to an equal syndrome; all seven cases recover the original data.)

> Two errors are miscorrected; three can be invisible

Now flip positions 1 and 2 of the original codeword. Syndrome 3 makes the
decoder flip position 3 as well. The result 1000011 is a different valid
codeword carrying 0011. The public field `corrected` remains true because a flip
was applied; it is not a verified recovery flag.

If the channel instead flips positions 1, 2, 3, the received 1000011 already has
zero syndrome. The decoder leaves it alone and returns 0011. Its `corrected`
field is false, although data are wrong. There is no universal error-count
classifier hidden in a three-bit syndrome.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hamming_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hamming_code_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::array<int, 4> data{1, 0, 1, 1};
    const auto code = dm::algorithms::hamming74_encode(data);

    dm::graphics::Diagram d(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 4.8;
    style.height = 1.8;
    style.font_size = 22;
    std::print("| flipped positions | syndrome | applied flip | returned data | recovered |\n| --- | ---: | " "--- | --- | --- |\n");
    for (int errors = 2; errors <= 3; ++errors) {
        auto r = code;
        for (int j = 0; j < errors; ++j)
            r[j] ^= 1;
        const auto t = dm::algorithms::hamming74_decode_trace(r);
        const auto &v = t.decoded.data;
        std::print("| 1..{} | {} | {} | {}{}{}{} | {} |\n", errors, t.syndrome,
                   t.decoded.corrected, v[0], v[1], v[2], v[3], v == data);
        const double y = errors == 2 ? 2. : -2.;
        const auto id = std::to_string(errors);
        d.node("in" + id, {0, y},
               std::format("Channel flips 1..{}\nReceived {}{}{}{}{}{}{}",
                           errors, r[0], r[1], r[2], r[3], r[4], r[5], r[6]),
               style).node("s" + id, {7, y},
                           std::format("Syndrome {}\nApplied flip: {}",
                                       t.syndrome, t.decoded.corrected), style).node("out" + id, {14, y}, std::format("Data {}{}{}{}\nRecovery: {}", v[0], v[1], v[2], v[3], v == data), style).edge("in" + id, "s" + id).edge("s" + id, "out" + id);
        if (v == data ||
            dm::algorithms::hamming74_syndrome(t.corrected_word) != 0)
            throw std::runtime_error("miscorrection controls");
    }
    d.text({7, 5}, "A codeword after decoding is not a recovery certificate", 25).save_svg("build/hamming_limits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| flipped positions | syndrome | applied flip | returned data | recovered |
| --- | ---: | --- | --- | --- |
| 1..2 | 3 | true | 0011 | false |
| 1..3 | 0 | false | 0011 | false |
\image(figures/hamming_limits.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Both paths return wrong data 0011 and a valid codeword. The two-error path applies a wrong correction; the three-error path has no detected syndrome.)
\alttext(Two rows contrast syndrome 3 after two errors with zero syndrome after three errors; both return data 0011 instead of 1011.)

Hamming(7,4) can detect any two errors if a nonzero syndrome is treated as a
rejection. This library interface instead always applies its one-error
correction rule. Simultaneously distinguishing single errors to correct from
double errors to reject requires more information. Adding an overall parity bit
yields an extended distance 4 code with such a policy, often called SECDED; that
eight-bit codec is not implemented or simulated here.

> Complete channel audit, then a stated probability model

The following table enumerates every transmitted word and every one of 128
binary error masks: 2048 cases. Weight is the number of flipped channel
positions, not the weight of the received word. No mask of weight 2 or greater
recovers the original data under this decoder: its output equals the original
codeword only when the received word is that codeword or one of its radius-one
neighbours.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hamming_channel.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hamming_code_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::array<int, 4> data{1, 0, 1, 1};
    const auto code = dm::algorithms::hamming74_encode(data);

    const auto a = dm::algorithms::hamming74_audit();
    std::print("| error weight | word-mask pairs | nonzero syndromes | original data recovered |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int w = 0; w <= 7; ++w) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", w, a.patterns[w],
                   a.nonzero_syndromes[w], a.recovered[w]);
        if (a.recovered[w] != (w <= 1 ? a.patterns[w] : 0))
            throw std::runtime_error("weight recovery");
    }
    std::vector<double> p, recovery;
    for (int j = 0; j <= 100; ++j) {
        const double v = j / 100.;
        p.push_back(v);
        recovery.push_back(dm::algorithms::hamming74_bsc_recovery(v));
    }
    std::print("\nIndependent BSC model at p=0.1: block recovery {:.7f}; p=0 recovery {}; p=1 recovery " "{}.\n",
               dm::algorithms::hamming74_bsc_recovery(.1),
               dm::algorithms::hamming74_bsc_recovery(0),
               dm::algorithms::hamming74_bsc_recovery(1));
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(p, recovery, "exact-model formula", {37, 99, 235}, 2.).title(
        "Independent equal-probability bit flips: block recovery").x_label(
        "per-bit flip probability p").y_label("probability all original data bits recover").save_svg("build/hamming_channel.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| error weight | word-mask pairs | nonzero syndromes | original data recovered |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 16 | 0 | 16 |
| 1 | 112 | 112 | 112 |
| 2 | 336 | 336 | 0 |
| 3 | 560 | 448 | 0 |
| 4 | 560 | 448 | 0 |
| 5 | 336 | 336 | 0 |
| 6 | 112 | 112 | 0 |
| 7 | 16 | 0 | 0 |

Independent BSC model at p=0.1: block recovery 0.8503056; p=0 recovery 1; p=1 recovery 0.
\image(figures/hamming_channel.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The table is a complete actual codec audit. The curve is a separately stated analytic channel model, rather than measured channel data or a Monte Carlo experiment.)
\alttext(Recovery decreases from one at zero flip probability to zero at one; the table distinguishes detection from correct recovery for all error weights.)

If seven bits flip independently with equal probability p, exact block recovery
is the event of zero or one flips:

$$P_{recover}=(1-p)^7+7p(1-p)^6.$$
\alttext(The binary symmetric channel model sums probabilities of zero errors and exactly one error.)

The curve evaluates this formula in double precision. It is not a confidence
interval or a claim about correlated, bursty or unequal hardware errors. At
p=0.1 it gives 0.8503056. Finite enumeration validates which patterns recover;
independence and equal p are additional model assumptions.

The full codeword weight enumerator is

$$A(z)=1+7z^3+7z^4+z^7.$$
\alttext(The code has one zero word, seven words of weight 3, seven of weight 4 and one of weight 7.)

These are also the zero-syndrome error shapes, since H(c XOR e)=He. For example,
among weight 3 errors,7 of 35 shapes are invisible to the syndrome. The other 28
give a nonzero syndrome and a miscorrection. None recovers the original data.

> Contracts, work and conclusions

All encoder, syndrome and decoder entries require array components exactly 0 or
1 and reject nonbinary values before correction indexing. The old decoder could
otherwise use an invalid computed syndrome as an array index. Valid binary
outputs and the three legacy decoded fields are preserved. Input arrays are
never mutated at the caller; trace arrays own their values, copy independently
and retain no references. Calls share no mutable state.

Encoding uses six parity XORs. Decoding's selected actual work is twelve parity
input visits, nine parity XORs, zero or one correction flips and four data
reads. Validation, weighted-syndrome assembly, snapshots, memory traffic and
machine operations are outside these selected counts. This fixed code needs
constant work and storage, with no heap or optional history cap; streaming B
independent blocks requires O(B) codec work, excluding external framing,
transmission and allocation costs. The exhaustive audit has fixed bounds of 16
words, 120 pairs and 2048 decodings, rather than an unbounded search interface.

The syndrome is powerful because distinct single errors have distinct check
patterns. The same perfect packing makes every received word look close to some
codeword, so an always-correct decoder cannot also certify recovery under
unrestricted noise. The original example proves its intended one-error behavior;
the complete audit and explicit counterexamples delimit that guarantee. An
unrelated dataset would add no evidence to this finite binary question.

The primary reference is R. W. Hamming (1950), [Error Detecting and Error
Correcting Codes](https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1950.tb00463.x), Bell
System Technical Journal 29, 147–160. Section 3 gives the checking-number
construction and power-of-two parity positions; section 4 distinguishes single
correction plus double detection with extra parity. This report uses that
position convention and the library's explicitly limited seven-bit decoder.

Verification checks all binary received words against an independently
enumerated parity-check codebook and unique nearest codeword, checks every
word/error-mask pair, and compares valid legacy outputs. Sanitizers cover
nonbinary rejection, ownership and actual XOR records. API examples,
language-server descriptions, all complete programs, inspected figures and both
exports are verified before the original report is retired.
